Bryan Bahnmiller <bbahnmil@redwood.dn.hac.com> writes:

>  We have a situation with a DEC 2100 running DU 4.0a running as an NFS
>server for a network. It's serving 3 12GB logical volumes over a FDDI
>connection to the backbone. The Sun clients are connected 10BaseT hubs
>or switches, with the switches having a FDDI connection to the backbone.
>Our Sun clients continually have problems with the DEC NFS mounts. We
>continually see: 

>NFS server alphasrv not responding still trying
>NFS server alphasrv ok

> DEC Unix clients work fine. 

How are the DEC clients connected?  This all sounds like network problems,
not unclean software.  The best thing to do is run snoop (on Solaris)
and tcpdump (on DEC) and try to look for places where messages are getting
lost.  The Digital UNIX FAQ has information about setting up tcpdump.

I've also used ping to find network lossage, but pretty much use tcpdump
exclusively these days.  You can also try FTPing large files to look
for throughput problems if you're interesting in absolving NFS of blaim.

nfsstat on the clients and netstat often provide very useful data.
