X-Complaints-To: Email abuse@news2.new-york.net if this posting is inappropriate
X-Trace: news2.new-york.net 891378968 20927 sysadmin [204.141.189.11]
Newsgroups: comp.sys.sun.admin,comp.unix.osf.osf1


If you are doing any kind of cross-network connections (FDDI to Ethernet),
then with NFS running over UDP one can hit
a problem with the differing MTU's on each network. When we last did tests of
this kind of setup, the only reliable way to go was
to explicitly set the MTU on the FDDI net to be the same small size as on
ethernet, and then the large UDP packets that NFS (in pre version 3) uses dont
get fragmented badly or discarded at the FDDI-Ethernet connection. Some
bridges can't do intelligent fragmenting.

I would not be surprised if your problems went away if you were running NFSV3
over TCP, or if you lower the MTU's on the machines on the FDDI side.

Oisin McGuinness
Sumitom Bank Capital Markets
277 Park Ave, NY, NY 10027
oisin@sbcm.com

