gustavo@fing.edu.uy wrote:
>  
> We cloned a workstation instead of installing it since  we thought
> this could simplify the tasks.
> The disk was originated on a DEC3000-300L with a video card of
> resolution 1024x768 and a color monitor
> The cloned disk was installed on a DEC 3000-300 but with a video
> card of 1280x1024 of resolution and a monochrome monitor.
> The machine boots fine but doesn't run the xdm (the monitor flashes a couple
> of times and returns to the ascii mode)
> I would be very thankful if someone can give me a hint
> of what the problem is? kernel, drivers or X?
> 

Cloning's a very convenient way to bring up a system but there are
a few steps to follow to make it go smoothly:

On Host System:

1.  Install target system's disk and use dump/restore or dd to make a
literal copy of the host.  When done, mount the target disk's root partition
somewhere which I'll call /<target_disk_mount>.

2.  In /<target_disk_mount>/etc/rc.config, *NOT* the host's /etc/rc.config,
modify HOSTNAME, IPCONFIG_*, PAGEFILE, and PARTITION as needed for the target.
'rcmgr' is a convenient way of doing this in a script.

3.  Modify /<target_disk_mount>/etc/fstab as needed.

4.  Add the target host address to /<target_disk_mount>/etc/hosts.

On Target System:

1.  Change the disk's unit number, install, and boot 'genvmunix' from console.

2.  As root, run 'doconfig' and boot the new kernel.  This step picks up the
new hardware, changes in slot assignments, different CPU/processor/chipset
support,
etc.

I've probably left off a few steps but that will get you very close.


