In article <351A971E.7DBC@hlthsrc.com>,
John McIntire  <mcintirj@hlthsrc.com> wrote:
> It is my understanding at this point the the only why to grow a file
> domain is by creating a volume (either striped or simple) and then
> adding it via the addvol command in advfs.

That is correct.  The LSM volume "growto" feature is only useful if you
have something on top of the volume that is extendable as well.  UFS
and AdvFS are not "growable", even when the underlying volume increases
in size.  The LSM growto feature would primarily be used by applications
that work with raw volumes and that can handle volumes that grow (such
as some databases, though going through LSM for a database probably isn't
a common practice), or filesystems that are growable.

You *can* extend the size of an AdvFS file domain by adding more volumes
to the file domains "free space pool".  There's nothing you can do for
UFS except recreate the filesystem.

Note that the Veritas filesystem, vxfs, is growable.  If Digital had
ported vxfs to Digital Unix like they did vxvm (aka LSM), you would have
a filesystem that was growable.  AdvFS is pretty cool (especially having
multiple file sets in a file domain), but vxfs is pretty cool too.

Tim
