Jenn-Huei Lii <robert@europa.chem.uga.edu> writes:

>Hi everybody,

>We have DEC AXP 3000-500 running OSF/1 v2.0.  Recently it keep saying
>that SWAP SPACE is less than 10% even when only few processes are
>running.  The only way I know is to kill the X-Window server process and
>restart it.  But sometimes I have to reboot the machine.  Does anyone
>kown what is happening? and how to fix the problem?

A common problem of the Xserver is that it grows over time and never
shrinks. So it is a good idea to have the Xserver restarted everytime
a user loggs off. This can be accomplished by adding the -terminate
switch to your Xserver-configuration file /var/X11/xdm/Xservers. It
should read like this:
:0 local /usr/bin/X11/X -nice -2 -terminate

Hope this helps,
   Wolfram

BTW:
   If you have a symbolic link named /sbin/swapdefault to the primary
swap device your system will be in eager allocation mode. By removing
this link you can force your sytem to overcommitment mode which will
reduce the amount of swap space warnings significantly. Read the
documentation on configuring your sytem for more information.

>Thanks in advance!

