My assumption is that "pbl" is the powerbuilder library, but it
doesn't really matter to the analysis. 

2nd assumption - the machine did not crash and is probably not
really hung. If the machine had crashed, your logs would have 
more info than you have listed here and you probably would not 
have had to cycle power. Just in case: Look in /var/adm/crash/ 
for recent crash data. To test whether the machine is 'hung' try 
logging into it across the net, pinging it, and try being very 
patient while waiting for keystrokes to echo. The machine could 
have a very greedy process and be spinning madly, but there is 
a real and useful difference between that and a real system hang. 

Your "Unaligned access" is probably only indirectly related to the
SEGV. Thus whoever was writing the bad code that results in the
"Unaligned access", probably put in a null pointer dereference too.
Find the pbl corefile, run dbx and use the 'where' cmd within dbx
to nail down which routine the SEGV occurred in.

Your next steps depend on what you find and how comfortable you
feel digging into the problem. A couple of references that might
be useful to you are in the Digital Unix Doc set: "Kernel Debugging"
and "System Tuning and Performance Management"

Email me if you like.

Charlie


