I just noticed this -- I've been assuming you meant to say
"...control whether stack or static memory is used at compile time".

F77 code can be trivially compiled to an executable that makes
no use whatsover of the stack *or* heap, and thus does not
grow during run time.  (Of course, the underlying system might
mandate certain uses of stack/heap for things like starting up
the program, calling the OS, etc., but it's unlikely that any
of this would contribute to overflowing the stack or heap when
run as part of a F77 program that is compiled to use neither!)
