Since Lynx 2.7 on up, there has been support for "new-style" client side
imagemaps (the type where the coodinates for the clickable areas and
their related URL's are defined within the HTML code itelf and no CGI
routines are involved). In such cases, Lynx will display the list of
links that image points to when selected (although there is no control
over what is displayed for the text as of yet).

Now, with a little care, it is even possible to make an "old-fashioned"
clickable imagemap (one dependent on a CGI routine) useable to ALL
browsers. Simply embed the imagemap within a "default" HREF. For
example:

	<A HREF="/cgi-bin/image map/pic2">
	<IMG SRC="../images/tech/pic2.gif" USEMAP="maps.html#map2" ISMAP>
	</A>

The outer HREF could point to the list of links related to the imagemap.
Works great as a way to allow more browsers access, and help those who
haven't quite mastered their mouse.

Things like this become very important when ADA compliance becomes more
pronounced (espcially if you are a government related agency). May I
suggest checking out some of the HTML newgroups?

In case you are wondering, most of this information can be found at:

	http://home.netscape.com/assist/net_sites/html_extensions_3.html



