Marc Thomas wrote:
> 
> We have several Alphastation 255's running Digital Unix 4.0 with no problems. Someone
> gave us an Alphastation 200 also running Digital Unix 4.0. We added the same software
> that we have on the Alphastation 255's and upgraded all licenses. However, use of
> either ladebug or gdb (by a non-privileged user) will usually cause a kernel panic
> on the Alphastation 200 (invalid instruction). These same debuggers and programs run
> fine on the other Alphastation 255's. I compared the kernel config files in /sys/conf
> and, allowing for a video board difference, they are essentially the same.
> 
> Before I wipe the disk and rebuild the Alphastation 200 from scratch, does anyone
> have any other ideas on what to check? Thanks in advance..
>

AKK !  No need for  that.  What you have is the infamous kernel debugger
bug.

Here's the fix I got from DEC:

The problem you are seeing with the system crashing with an
"illegal instruction" when running the debugger is caused by a kernel
configuration problem present in Digital UNIX 4.0-4.0b, to work around
this
problem you must turn off kernel debugging by either rebuilding the
kernel
using doconfig or by following the procedure outlined below:

The workaround -  set db_enable to 0.

To fix the running kernel

        # dbx -k /vmunix
        # assign db_enable = 0
        # q

To fix the disk copy (so you don't have to redo the live kernel after a
reboot)

        # dbx /vmunix          /* Important!  No -k here
        # patch db_enable = 0
        # q

Of course, if you rebuild your kernel, you should take kdebug out of the
options in the config file first, or do the last step to the new /vmunix
after the fact.



