> 
> 
> <MAP NAME="top">
[...]
> </MAP>
> 
> Are there *any* character only browsers that can not deal with 
> this.  

"Character only" is a complete red herring.  The only issue is support
for client-side imagemaps--graphical vs. character has nothing to do
with whether the site is navigable or not.

For a list of browser support of client-side imagemaps, see

http://www.browsercaps.com/config/Sv/Generated/test-clientmap.html

The list of "no" entries is pretty short--Mosiac, Chimera, some old
versions of lynx--but non-empty, and some people must use those old
browsers or there never would have been a complaint (and I do see
signifcant numbers of hits on our web server from Mosaic in
particular).  Digital could have made the site navigable for browsers
that don't support client-side imagemaps, but chose not to, thus
making the site impossible to navigate for a small fraction of their
audience, including some using fully compliant HTML 2.0 browsers.
(I'm also dismayed by the proliferation of JavaScript on Digital's
site--anyone with *any* concerns about security ought to have
JavaScript disabled by default in their browser.)

> Jonathan has  a " port of XMosaic 2.7 to VMS with
> considerable input from the French mmosaic effort" that does
> "most of the HTML 3.2".  I don't think that Mosaic development
> survived into the HTML3.2/imagemap era. So unless someone has
> a better browser for VMS ...  hmmm... sort of says something
> about VMS...   Is there a Java port to VMS?

Netscape is available for VMS, presumably from Digital, and there's
a beta of JDK 1.1.5 for OpenVMS Alpha at:

http://www.digital.com/java/new/jdkovms_new.html

No idea about VAX support, but if you want a real orphan, look at
Ultrix.
