In article <6eopju$pfi$2@news.utrecht.NL.net>, Toon Moene <toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> writes:
|> [ I hope I can add some bits to this discussion, but might
|>   raise more questions instead ... ]
|>
|> Peter Shenkin <shenkin@still3.chem.columbia.edu> wrote:
|>
|> [ ... about the allocation of automatic arrays ... ]
|>
|> > One of these was a compile-time flag for a size threshold to force
|> > allocation of large automatics on the heap.  In this scenario, the
|> > program writer knowingly and explicitly chooses to trade speed
|> > for reliablility.  Yes, this requires a runtime test, but the
|> > Cray isn't exactly a slouch when it comes to execution speed, and
|> > it is Cray who implements this feature.
|>
|> It's not suprising Cray implemented such a feature, because, AFAIK, Cray PVP 
|> systems do not have a stack as such - the "stack" is also managed as a heap.  
|> Perhaps Jeff Drummond or one of the other Crayons listening in can elaborate 
|> on this point.

Yes, that is correct.  On Cray PVP systems the stack is just a flock of
heap segments that have been wrestled to the ground and lashed together
with a bunch of #8 pointers.  The process of pushing anything on the
stack requires a not insignificant amount of code to (a) check if there's
room in the current segment and, if not, (b) allocate a new segment and
link it to the existing segment(s).  [This is, incidently, what makes
implementing alloca() on Cray PVP systems an ultimate exercise in
futility.]

One consequence of all this is that as the size of your allocatable
object increases, it actually becomes easier (i.e., faster) to allocate
it directly from the heap rather than on the stack (which is not the
case on most architectures).  Thus, the option to force allocation of
large automatics on the heap is not quite the trade of speed for
reliability that Mr. Shenkin supposes.  Rather, it was probably created
as a performance enhancement (the error message you get is the same in
either case).

But regardless of all these behind-the-scenes machinations, the basic
plea for getting a descriptive error message when something goes
wrong is certainly understandable.  Two points:

1) Doing graceful error recovery when you run out of memory (especially
   stack space) can be problematic.

2) Most newer architectures allow the actual mechanics of memory
   allocation to be hidden in the operating system.  They have
   segmented memory mapping which permits the OS to expand memory
   "invisibly" by trapping segment violations and making additional
   memory segments available.  This means that expanding the stack
   typically reduces to a simple register increment, and makes stack
   pushes/pops trivial for the implementor (compare and contrast
   with the Cray PVP method I outlined above).

   Thus, allocating memory in this fashion doesn't really do anything
   (that is, nothing is actually allocated--and won't be unless/until
   it is actually referenced).  This is fine as long as the memory
   will be available when needed, but otherwise the program will
   likely be far, far away from the point of allocation when the
   error finally comes home to roost.  Hence, the ubiquitous
   'segmentation fault' error message you see on so many systems.

   Of course, there's no reason why a library or OS primitive couldn't
   be provided to ensure that X amount of memory was actually present
   before proceding (or return an error indication if it was not
   available).  This would at least allow memory allocation "routines"
   to issue a descriptive error message.

-Jeff    jjd@cray.com
--
"Segmentation fault
Insufficient memory
You lose, plum blossom."
-Haikunix 3.2 error message



