> 
> You're quite right in that other things can blow the stack
> besides automatic variables.  But the advent of automatics
> makes the ability to handle stack overflow *in their particular
> case* especially important in practice.  The fact is that a
> stack overflow has gone from being very rare to rather likely,
> in my experience, when automatics are used.  If we handle the
> most common cases -- like failure of automatic allocation --
> explicitly, then maybe it's not too bad to leave everything
> else in the same "miscellaneous" category where they've always
> lived, relatively immune from complaint.  I agree that an attempt
> to handle all causes explicitly leads to a morass.

Yes, well, it occurred to me later that the history of this
area in Fortran probably includes the fact that, indeed, for
"classic Fortran", stack overflow was rarely a problem, and
that the introduction of automatic arrays was the *single*
biggest reason why it might become one.

Similarly, heap overflow was rarely a problem for "classic
Fortran".  Even if the underlying run-time libraries (provided
with the compiler) used heap (or stack) storage, the demands
of "classic Fortran" applications were probably such that these
wouldn't contribute noticably to exhausting these particular
resources.

In particular, this stems from the lack of powerful facilities
provided by "classic Fortran", by which I should say I mean
FORTRAN 77, or earlier, with "typical" extensions -- ones that
don't demand dynamic allocation, in the various intrinsics,
I/O, and other "implicit" facilities provided by the language.

Essentially, using FORTRAN 77, it is fairly straightforward to
analyze a complete program and determine whether it will run
to completion in a given amount of memory, because there's
no ability to explicitly *or* implicitly demand dynamic
allocation of memory resources (stack or heap).

It stands to reason, then, that, as *explicit* facilities were
added as extensions (or new features in F90), people would assume
that those were suitable places on which to "hang" the ability
to catch memory exhaustion.

After all, if stack/heap overflow wasn't a problem for "classic
Fortran", and it might be for "classic Fortran plus ALLOCATE"
(or whatever), then the "obvious" conclusion is "well, give the
programmer a way to catch a failure to ALLOCATE, because that's
where memory exhaustion will happen".

However, now Fortran (90, 95, F2K, etc.) have significant and
extensive facilities that demand dynamic allocation, both explicit
*and* implicit (e.g. dynamic character allocation, dynamic array
allocation on assignments, etc.).

At this point, then, while I agree there is still some practical
benefit to allowing catching of out-of-memory conditions on
*explicit* allocations, I feel this benefit diminishes as the
application makes more use of F90-and-beyond features.

That is, stick to "classic Fortran plus ALLOCATE" or similar, and
catching out-of-memory still makes sense the way its done now.

But, as more applications make full use of Fortran features that
do *implicit* allocations, the utility of detecting an out-of-memory
condition on only *explicit* allocations decreases, because an
explicit allocation can succeed only to have a subsequent implicit
allocation fail, with no way to catch it using the current
language facilities.  And, since that likely means it's the
preceding explicit allocation that "strained" resources such
that the implicit allocation couldn't succeed, the application
does not get the feedback it needs that the explicit allocation
could not, in effect, proceed without the application crashing
unexpectedly.

My concern is that too many programmers will code as if catching
out-of-memory on explicit allocations (even assuming we extended
this, as I think perhaps we should, to automatic arrays and other
stack allocations) is a panacea.  That means their applications
might be *coded* so that they'll never truly run out of memory
in a way that they can't catch and handle.

And, what's particularly dangerous about that assumption is that
they'll be correct when their code is the only "fancy" code in
an application, but the assumption's correctness will degrade,
perhaps to the point of being almost certainly incorrect, as
*other* code in the same application is rewritten to take advantage
of newer features.

To look at this problem in a different light, imagine how programmers
would feel if they discovered that, sometimes, the underlying
run-time library would arbitrarily decide to write a large
temporary file, and if it ran out of disk space doing that, it
would just crash the application without any opportunity to catch
the error.

In that context, programmers would justifiably complain that
their *code* was written to assume that, as long as it caught
all possible I/O errors via IOSTAT= or ERR=, it wouldn't be
crashed by a disk-full condition.  Yet, their applications
could be randomly crashed by disk-full in a way that was basically
impossible to predict, especially in portable code.  In fact,
that sort of thing, while unlikely, would seem to be perfectly
standard-conforming behavior.

However, this illustration points out how troubling it might
become that applications, written to increasingly depend upon
heap and stack allocation to run reliably, find they are
thus increasingly likely to crash because the run-time system
is quietly competing with the explicit code for those resources --
with no way to catch the cases where the resources are exhausted
due to one of these implicit allocations, even though all
explicit allocations might well be coded to catch out-of-memory
conditions and handle them gracefully!

It's not that I've run into this problem in practice with Fortran,
yet, since I don't really deal with F90 and beyond.  But, I always
try to do thought-exercises regarding any new language features,
system features, implementation decisions (or hacks ;-), and so
on, thinking through how they might interact, for better or worse,
with each other as they're more accepted and thus become more
thoroughly exploited.  And my thought-exercises regarding all
these features suggest that some of them (say, catching out-of-
memory conditions on explicit allocations) become useless
appendages, or worse, when they are exploited along with other
features.  That doesn't mean they aren't useful in "smaller
groups" of features being employed by a particular application,
however.
