I'm not so sure it isn't (or won't be) a bottleneck.  There are lots 
of real codes that call a very small procedure gazillions of times.
Call overhead often dominates the run-time of the small procedure.
Fortran 90 potentially makes this worse with the ideas of 
encapsulation and information hiding and user defined operators.  
Lots of little routines that effectivelly do a couple of loads, an add,
and a store.  Adding several cycles to the entry and exit sequence
can have a big effect.

This doesn't mean compilers shouldn't do it, only that there is a
real cost.  Inlining helps, but some library code is only available
in binary form.

Dick Hendrickson



