Dick Hendrickson <dick.hendrickson@worldnet.att.net> writes:

> This doesn't mean compilers shouldn't do it, only that there is a
> real cost.  Inlining helps, but some library code is only available
> in binary form.

That's a point in favor of short-term practicality, but I'd
like to point out that restricting language and tool design to
allow library code to be distributed only in binary form
(i.e. proprietary and made hard to reverse engineer), especially
to *existing* such libraries, *severely* restricts the power of
those languages and tools.

For example, I've observed (somewhat casually ;-) the long-term
efforts, and problems, incurred trying to put decent exception
handling into gcc, and it seems that most of the actual problems,
and thus effort resolving them, revolves around catering to
already-compiled binary code, where source is inconvenient or
unavailable.

I think it's not worth catering to these needs at all, or at a
very low priority, in new-language design, and leave it to the
vendors of proprietary libraries and relevant tools to improve
*their* products to meet *their* requirements as well as the
(new) ones of the language environment.  (But I'm afraid too
many of them would prefer the language be crippled for non-
proprietary users to make their proprietary tools easier to build.)
