nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Nick Maclaren) writes:

> However, one rare construction in Fortran 77 (character concatenation)
> can theoretically lead to a program with O(N) total allocated space
> needing O(2^N) to run.  Which is, of course, why almost all compilers
> have an ad-hoc kludge or two for the space to use for that horrible
> construction :-(

Eh?  Are you talking about standard f77 or some extension?  Standard
Fortran 77 goes out of its way to disallow the cases where concatenation
would otherwise require dynamic allocation.  This is exactly the reason
for a couple of the otherwise inexplicable limitations... such as

  subroutine sub(c)
    character c*(*)
    character c1*10
    c1 = c // "a"  !-- This is ok (except for this form of commenting).
    call sub2( c // "a" )  !-- But this isn't.
  end

If you know a case where standard f77 requires dynamic allocation, I'd sure
like to see a sample - obscure or not.

