In article <3506AEAF.2A706FF4@netvigator.com>, David Lee <davidskl@netvigator.com> writes:
|> A lot of TIME_WAIT means the three way TCP hand shaking doesn't complete.

Not exactly.  Digital UNIX can keep zillions of TIME_WAIT entries
(as long as you have enough RAM, and the original poster had 2GB)
without affecting its ability to accept new connections.

If indeed the "three-way handshake" (SYN packet exchange) is failing,
this is because the "socket listen queue depth" is too small.  The
default on V4.0 is, I believe, 1024; you can set it as high as 32767.

For full instructions, and much more about tuning Digital UNIX
for high-performance Web servers, please see:

    http://www.digital.com/internet/document/ias/tuning.html

|> You have to decrease the tcp_keepinit to 30.

This should not be necessary if the system is configured according
to the document cited above.  However, if your system is under
a "SYN-flooding" attack, it *might* help to reduce tcp_keepinit.

By the way, large numbers of TIME_WAIT states are entirely
normal; in fact, if your server is busy, this is basically
required by the TCP specification (RFC793).

-Jeff



