Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Karl,

LSM and AdvFS can work together in order to get the best performance and
fexilibity. Both provide a good management tools. For example, you can
create
a LSM volume and using the volume to create a AdvFS domain. The LSM volume
can be in mirror, stripe..etc. Also you can uses LSM to create a BIG
volume so it not just limited by the physical disk size. Using AdvFS you
can
increase the file system size on fly, perform defragmentation or online
backup
which we can clone set. Also, you can uses LSM feature to increase the LSM
volume size on fly but it limited on RAW device (no file system on top). If
you
have file system on top of LSM volume and you want ot increase volume size
by using LSM command "volassist grow". You may need to backup your file
system first and then increase the volume size and recreate your file
domain.

Attached are the advantage and overivew for LSM and AdvFS.

Logical Storage Manager (LSM) provides tools to more effectively
manage disk storage in today's demanding storage environment. Using
virtual disks, called volumes, LSM eases the difficulty of managing
traditional UNIX[R] storage, especially for growing commercial systems
with tens or hundreds of disks. Instead of using inadequate tools to
manage standard physical devices, LSM gives you flexible tools to
manage logical volumes consisting of arbitrary areas of disks. Because
these volumes transcend the limitations of native UNIX partitions and
devices, LSM gives you more flexibility to mold storage resources
according to the needs of applications, databases, and users. Disks can
be added to or removed from volumes without shutting down the system.
Spanning builds virtual disks larger than the underlying physical disks.
Mirroring provides redundant data if a disk fails, without interrupting
active use of the system. Striping increases input/output (I/O)
performance.

The Logical Storage Manager provides software volume (block and raw
devices) abstraction with DIGITAL UNIX. LSM provides:

    Data Spanning and partitioning
    Data Mirroring (RAID1)
    Data Striping (RAID0)
    Combined mirroring of a stripe set (RAID0 and RAID1)
    Comprehensive ONLINE data storage management:
        Add and remove disks
        Reconfigure volumes to change configuration
        Migrate data for disk repair or load balancing while data is in use

        Monitor Performance
        GUI and command line interfaces


AdvFS Overview

Maximize Availability of Data - minimize file systemdown time

    Fast crash / powerfail recovery by using a
    transactions to log metadata modifications
    On-line file system management:
        Backup
        Adding and removing volumes
        Defragmenting, capacity balancing and load
        balancing

Ease of use (as compared to logical volume managers)

    Simple to change configuration (addvol, rmvol)
    Minimize new concepts
    Use files rather than volumes as the way to manage
    the file system. This can be seen in the per-file
    striping support vs. volume striping (per-file mirroring
    was also planned) and in the way removing a
    volume can be done by migrating the file to another
    volume and the way defragment and balance use
    migrate to move files around.

Maximize Performance - minimize disk I/O's

    Extent-based allocations to allow for large contiguous I/O's
    Multi-volume support to allow for striping and load balancing
    Transaction logging to remove need for synchronous metadata updates

Hope this help.

David Lee.

