> I have done some performance testing on a Digital Alpha running Digital
> UNIX 4.0.  When I compile my test program (which is simply a C++ string
> class and a main test program) with the -threads option, it takes up
> 600% more CPU time than when I omit the -threads option. Note that I am
> not doing anything multi-threaded in my test, just using the -threads
> switch.
> 
> I can uderstand if it were 10% different, but 600% ?!
> 
> Does anyone out there who has experience with MT programs on this
> platform have any info to share on this situation?  Can what I'm seeing
> actually be true, or am I completely in the weeds?
When you compile with threads, that makes your program a threaded
application. There is some extra overhead that will be generated. Also,
all the routines in libc are thread-safe, so you get some overhead, such
as the creation of mutexes. Also, there are a signiificant number of
changes to threads between the versions of 4.0. 
