In article <6d3o77$g21$1@nnrp2.dejanews.com>, matsjo@ainigma.com writes:
|> Doing some networking related benchmarking on Digital UNIX 4.0B. Seems that
|> something limits the number of tcp connections the box can handle to
|> exactly 100 connections/second.
|> 
|> Anyone aware of any kernel/internal parameters which fixes this.

Getting good TCP performance often requires understanding a lot
of complex details.  (For example, using the write system calls
and buffer sizes.)  It's more than I can possibly describe in
a short message, especially without seeing your source code.

However, one should easily be able to get 10 times as many TCP
connections per second.  For example, published benchmark results
for SPECWeb96 on a 1-CPU Alphaserver 1200 5/533 show 1631 HTTP
requests (= TCP connections, in this benchmark) per second.  See
  http://www.specbench.org/osg/web96/results/res97q4/web96-971103-02229.html

Note that this result was obtained with V4.0D, which might help
your numbers somewhat, but V4.0B should easily exceed 100 connections
per second.

For some tuning information, check out
   http://www.digital.com/internet/document/ias/tuning.html

-Jeff



