posselt@i40a6.ira.uka.de (Dietmar Posselt) writes:

>We have 2 new Alphas running OSF4.0B. Every once in a while the machines
>seem to be under a heavy load for no reason. We guess that this happens due
>to a currently inaccessible NFS directory, but other reasons are also
>possible.

If I think it's network related, I reach for tcpdump.  The Digital UNIX FAQ
has information on setting up the packet filter and other things you need to
do.

Instead of tcpdump, you may get some information from netstat and nfsstat,
or by watching syd or top (freeware) or repeated "ps alxm" runs.

If you think it's automount, you can try running it with "-T -T" to watch
its behaviour, but be careful, it's easy to hang the system if you
don't stop automount gracefully.  The only safe way is to use kill -TERM.

You're much better off starting with tcpdump - I use it every day.
